  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nEarly morning surface analysis shows a cool, dry post-frontal air \nmass firmly entrenched across Kansas. A surface ridge axis continues\nto build southward into the Central Plains early this morning, \nsupporting light north to northeast winds around 5 mph or less and \nefficient radiational cooling. \n\nDespite abundant sunshine today, continued cool low-level air and\nweak mixing beneath the surface ridge will hold afternoon highs in\nthe upper 40s to near 50 degrees, roughly 5 to 10 degrees below\nmid-November climatological normals. Winds remain light from the\nnorth to northeast at 5 to 10 mph. As the surface high settles\ndirectly over eastern Kansas tonight into Saturday morning, light \nwinds and a dry boundary layer will set up another night of strong \nradiational cooling. Saturday morning looks to be the colder of the \ntwo mornings, with widespread lows in the low to mid 20s, \nparticularly in sheltered valleys and along the Kansas and Republican\nRiver basins. \n\nOver the weekend, an elongated western upper-level trough splits, \ncarving out a closed mid-level low that tracks eastward across the \nOklahoma and Texas Panhandles by early Sunday. Earlier model spread \nregarding the northern extent of precipitation has trended \nconsistently southward, keeping deeper Gulf moisture and forcing well\nsouth of the Kansas-Oklahoma border. Meanwhile, low-level ridging \nanchored east of the forecast area maintains dry easterly to \nsoutheasterly low-level flow across the area. Expect an increase in \nmid and high-level clouds Saturday afternoon into Saturday night, \nbut dry weather is expected to prevail through Sunday. Daytime highs \nremain cool in the upper 40s to low 50s through the weekend.\n\nLooking into early and mid-next week, upper-level heights rise \nbehind the departing southern trough as surface high pressure shifts \neast toward the Mississippi Valley. Developing southerly return flow \nwill promote steady warm air advection across Kansas, lifting \nafternoon highs through the 50s early next week and back to near 60 \ndegrees by Wednesday, alongside milder overnight lows above \nfreezing.\n\nBy late next week, medium-range guidance signals the next upper- \nlevel trough approaching from the western CONUS. While this system \nintroduces the next potential window for rainfall across northeast \nand east-central Kansas, significant uncertainty remains regarding \ntrough amplitude, progression speed, and the quality of boundary- \nlayer moisture return ahead of the system. Accordingly, precipitation\nchances remain low and broad-brushed at this range.",
